  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Trade Union Confederation of Moldova (CNSM) submitted proposals for consideration by the Government regarding the labour inspection law amendments in Moldova. The new law, introduced in November 2020, reflected the trade union positions and will provide workers with increased OSH protection.

Through the social dialogue mechanism at national level, CNSM has promoted and proposed ratification of ILO Convention No. 161 on Occupational Health Services. In March 2021, the Moldovan Parliament approved in the final reading the draft law for the ratification of the Convention which will enter into force for the Republic of Moldova in May 2022.

In mid-2020 the CNSM developed proposals and effectively engaged with the Government and employers on stimulus packages during the COVID-19 pandemic. Through tripartite dialogue at national level, the CNSM was able to change the COVID-19 response package adopted. For example, in mid-2020, the trade union input changed the Government approach to COVID-19 testing of workers.  A new law on the amendments to the fiscal code (nr 1163/199) was introduced (mid-2020), under which employers are to fund COVID-19 tests of workers, while the Government allow employers tax reductions to cover the costs. 

CNSM proposed to establish tripartite local economic committees for Cantemir and Causeni districts. The proposals were accepted at national level, and the committees, tasked with tripartite consultation and collective bargaining at district level, were established in the first quarter of 2021. ILO provided advice and guidance to the National Trade Union Confederation of Moldova (CNSM) on International Labour Standards, COVID-19 responses and social dialogue. 

In the first half of 2020, ILO organized four online webinars with the aim to assist the union in developing proposals and engaging with the Government and employers on stimulus packages, OSH policies, adopting work arrangements, etc. 

In 2020, ILO organized trade union meetings in nine districts in Moldova and initiated and funded a training of trainers programme by the union. The aim was to broaden trade union membership and for the union to become more relevant to more groups of workers.  In the activities, the union targeted workers in areas where trade unions are traditionally weak: agriculture and informal economy. 

Through RBSA intervention, ILO provided technical assistance to CNSM using the ILO tool &quot;Rights4Youth@Work&quot;. The tool includes the description of the labour legislation of Moldova, OSH regulations and provisions on how to receive assistance in specific circumstances. Through the activities, the trade union proposed setting up new local tripartite bodies in Cantemir and Causeni.</narrative>
   </description>
